---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="About" description="What UAP Times is, and the editorial standard we hold ourselves to.">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="article-header prose">
      <h1>About UAP Times</h1>
      <p class="lede">Serious reporting on an unserious-sounding subject.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="article-body prose">
      <p>
        UAP Times covers unidentified anomalous phenomena — UFOs, if you're
        feeling old-fashioned — the way any other news beat should be covered:
        with sources, scepticism, and a clear line between what's known and
        what's merely claimed.
      </p>
      <h5>Our editorial standard</h5>
      <p>
        We don't debunk for sport and we don't believe for sport either. Every
        story carries a credibility rating so you always know how much weight the
        evidence actually bears. A government sensor log and a blurry phone video
        are not the same thing, and we won't pretend they are.
      </p>

      <h5 id="how-we-rate">The credibility scale</h5>
      <p>
        There's really only one scale here, and it's a <strong>sourcing</strong>
        scale. Every story carries a credibility rating shown as five dots — read
        it like marks out of five, where <strong>more filled dots means
        better-sourced</strong>. It measures the strength of the evidence, not
        whether we think it's aliens: a well-sourced case can still turn out
        mundane, and a "take with salt" one might still be real.
      </p>
      <div class="cred-key">
        {[
          { n: 5, label: 'Well-sourced', desc: "Primary documents or sensor data we've been able to examine ourselves." },
          { n: 4, label: 'Credible', desc: 'Multiple reliable witnesses, or official acknowledgement of the event.' },
          { n: 3, label: 'Unconfirmed', desc: 'A genuine report, but corroboration is thin or second-hand.' },
          { n: 2, label: 'Thin', desc: 'Single-source, or with shaky provenance.' },
          { n: 1, label: 'Take with salt', desc: 'An uncorroborated claim — interesting, but not evidence.' },
        ].map((r) => (
          <div class="cred-key-row">
            <span class="cred-key-dots" aria-label={`${r.n} out of 5`}>
              {'●'.repeat(r.n)}<span class="off">{'○'.repeat(5 - r.n)}</span>
            </span>
            <span class="cred-key-label">{r.label}</span>
            <span class="cred-key-desc">{r.desc}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p>
        You'll see the dots on every article card and a word (like "Credible") on
        the article itself; on the <a href="/map">sightings map</a> the same rating
        sets each contact's colour — red is best-sourced, fading to grey for the
        doubtful — and drives the filters.
      </p>

      <h5>How we work</h5>
      <p>
        Stories are researched and drafted with AI assistance and reviewed by a
        human before publication. We think that's the honest way to run a modern
        newsroom, and we'd rather tell you than have you guess.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
